<h3 class="storytitle" id="post-22"><a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?p=22" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Friendship">Friendship</a></h3>
<!-- Post meta -->
<div class="meta">
Posted on June 9th, 2004. <!-- By Mark. --> About <a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?cat=3" title="View all posts in The Narrow Path">The Narrow Path</a>. <a href="http://www.marksportal.net/blog/index.php?p=22#comments">No Comments</a>.</div>

<!-- Actual post -->
<div class="storycontent">
	<p>Last night I saw parts of the movie &#8220;Emma&#8221;.  I had seen the movie before and, much to my surprise, really enjoyed it.  I&#8217;m not really sure why.  Maybe it was the way that they spoke&#8230;.paying extreme attention to the words they used and not having as much outward emotion.   Personally, I don&#8217;t believe this is something that I could do, but it&#8217;s interesting to watch. </p>
	<p>Anyways, back to the subject at hand.  As I was saying, I was watching parts of &#8220;Emma&#8221; last night and happened to catch one of my favorite scenes in the movie.  A bunch of people were having a picnic and playing games.   It was a fun atmosphere.   As they were talking about the rules of a game Emma said something rude to one of the poor, elderly ladies that was with them.  The elderly lady was hurt and the younger Mr. Knightly caringly took her for a walk to try and take her out of the situation, as well as take her mind off the comment.   Later on Mr. Knightly meets up with Emma and reprimanded her for the comment.   It&#8217;s his departing words to Emma that makes this movie so enjoyable to me.   After reprimanding Emma, he says to her with wisdom, kindness, and love:</p>
	<p><em>&#8220;It is not pleasant for me to say these things, but I must tell you the truth while I still can, proving myself your friend by the most faithful counsel, trusting that sometime you will do my faith in you greater justice that you do it now.&#8221;</em></p>
	<p>&#8220;Proving myself your friend by the most faithful counsel&#8221;.   That line is so powerful to me.  By reprimanding her, he had hoped to weed out the insensitivity that she had shown toward the elderly woman.  He was not reprimanding her just because what she did was wrong but because he did not want her going down the wrong path and in so doing would prove himself to be her friend.   Not proving himself her friend by mere words, but in deeds&#8230;.by giving her faithful counsel.  It reminds me a verse in Proverbs:</p>
	<p><em>&#8220;Wounds from a friend can be trusted, but an enemy multiplies kisses.&#8221; (Proverbs 27:6, NIV)</em></p>
	<p>Anyone can say kind words and throw praises upon you.   A true friend is one that also provides trustworthy counsel, be it unpleasent or not.  They are honest with you and excerise their love the most when they confront you on something no one else will.  This confronting is not done in anger or harsh words.  It&#8217;s done with tenderness, love, and compassion.   The Bible has other words on friendship:</p>
	<p><em>&#8220;A friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.&#8221; (Proverbs 17:17, NIV)</p>
	<p>&#8220;A man of many companions may come to ruin, but there is a friend who sticks closer than a brother.&#8221; (Proverbs 18:24, NIV)</p>
	<p>&#8220;Greater love has no one than this, that he lay down his life for his friends.&#8221; (John 15:13, NIV)</em></p>
	<p>Many people do not want to confront a friend for fear of the loss they might incur, be it loss of friendship or some emotional harm to themselves.  This is the place I sit as I try to become a better friend.    It&#8217;s a barrier that is hard to pass over, yet John 15:13 is very clear.  If you love your friend, lay down your life, lay down your fears, lay down the concerns you have for yourself and speak to them in love.
</p>
</div>			
